{
  "file": "crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/language.rs",
  "item": "fn[whole_comment]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This callback fills the Profile interface for every non-Rust profile.\n Those profiles all set hashes_comment to never, so Builder::encode skips\n comment_prose for every source input; plain text does not use the grammar\n encoder at all. Rust hashes doc comments through its separate doc_prose.\n The fallback therefore has no reachable behavior in the supported profiles."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/language.rs#fn[whole_comment]` is sound. It is written as fenced commentary on an explicit function, which is the permitted form. The reason it gives is substantive, and it matches the current source.\n\n- **Only one caller.** The only call to `comment_prose` in the crate is in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/mod.rs#impl[Builder<'tree,%20'source>]/fn[encode]`. That call sits behind `if (self.profile.hashes_comment)(node, self.source)`.\n- **`never` is always false.** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/language.rs#fn[never]` returns `false`, so a profile that uses it never reaches the `comment_prose` call.\n- **Every user of `whole_comment` uses `never`.** The profiles that set `comment_prose` to `whole_comment` are css, markdown, python, toml, typescript and yaml (each under `outline/languages/`). All of them set `hashes_comment: never`. The plain-text profile in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/plain.rs#static[PROFILE]` does the same, and it also has `language: None`. The comment's claim that plain text does not use the grammar encoder is therefore consistent with the source, and it doesn't change the conclusion either way.\n- **Rust is separate.** Rust sets `hashes_comment: is_doc_comment` and `comment_prose: doc_prose`, so it never uses `whole_comment`.\n\nThe fallback therefore has no reachable behavior in the supported profiles, as the commentary says. This is a reason specific to the code, not just the difficulty of testing it. Nothing in the source contradicts it.",
